From d5b7b57be2fed323d5d701b25021ffe0a8c4052f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sylvestre Ledru <sylvestre@debian.org>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 22:36:48 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] preprocessor cache: remember file digests across
 direct-mode lookups

On every direct-mode cache hit, the server re-read and re-hashed the source
file and every header it includes to check that none had changed. That was
most of its work on a hit: blake3, the time macro scan and the file reads
together took ~70% of the server's CPU.

The server is long-lived, so keep each file's digest and time macro flags in
memory, and reuse them while the file's size, mtime, ctime, device and inode
are unchanged, as ccache's inode cache does. A file is only remembered if its
timestamps are at least two seconds old when it is read, since they come
from a coarse clock and might not tell a quick rewrite apart, and if its stat
did not change during the read. This is Unix only: on Windows there is no
inode, and the creation time standing in for the ctime is not updated by a
rewrite.

Server CPU per direct-mode cache hit (perf stat, 200 hits):
  C:                    0.97 ms -> 0.43 ms (-56%)
  C++ (STL headers):    4.06 ms -> 0.89 ms (-78%)

End to end (hyperfine, 100 runs):
  C direct-mode hit:    2.6 ms -> 1.8 ms
  C++ direct-mode hit:  7.3 ms -> 3.2 ms

Signed-off-by: Javier Tia <javier@peridio.com>
